from pathlib import Path

root=Path(__file__).parent
deep={
'part-01-problem.html':'''<h2>Start with the everyday picture</h2><p>Imagine a lender looking at a person who already has a credit card. The lender is not trying to read that person's mind. It has a small record of what happened before: the credit limit, the bills sent over recent months, amounts paid, and whether payments were late. The question in this dataset is very narrow: based on those old records, did the client default on the payment due next month?</p><p>"Prediction" does not mean certainty. If the model gives 0.70, it is placing the profile on the high end of its learned risk scale. The current project has not checked whether a 0.70 score is perfectly calibrated, so do not translate that score into a promise that exactly seven out of ten similar people will default.</p><h3>Why labels matter</h3><p>A computer cannot learn this problem from inputs alone. It needs past examples where the answer is known. Each old row says, in effect, "this client had these circumstances, and the recorded next-month outcome was 0 or 1." During training, the model looks for input patterns that tend to appear more often before a 1 than before a 0. At prediction time, the target column is absent. Giving it the answer would be cheating.</p><h3>A careful way to talk about benefit</h3><p>A risk estimate could help staff decide which accounts to look at first. It cannot tell us why a client may struggle, whether they deserve credit, or what a fair decision would be. In real lending, people need clear policies, review, privacy protection, legal controls, and a chance to correct mistakes. This repository is a learning project that demonstrates the technical path from data to a score.</p>''',
'part-02-dataset.html':'''<h2>How to read one row</h2><p>Think of a row as one client snapshot. <code>LIMIT_BAL</code> is the approved ceiling in New Taiwan dollars. <code>BILL_AMT1</code> is the most recent statement balance, while <code>PAY_AMT1</code> is the amount paid for that period. The six PAY columns describe repayment status over time. A recent delay can be more informative than a bill by itself because it records whether payment obligations were met.</p><p>The suffixes are easy to mix up. In this data, 1 is the most recent month for bill/payment amounts, then 2, 3 and so on go backward. The repayment columns have the unusual names PAY_0, PAY_2, PAY_3 through PAY_6. That naming comes from the source data; the active project keeps it. Do not silently rename a column unless every input path, trained artifact and test is updated too.</p><h3>Categories versus numbers</h3><p>SEX is stored as 1 or 2, but adding or averaging those values has no natural meaning. EDUCATION and MARRIAGE work the same way. They are codes for groups. By contrast, age, bill amount and payment amount have numerical size, so operations such as mean and standard deviation make sense. Repayment-status codes sit in the middle: the project treats them as ordered numerical values because higher positive delay codes represent more delay. That is a modelling decision worth discussing in an interview.</p><h3>What the raw checks tell us</h3><p>Zero missing cells and zero duplicate rows are good starting facts, not proof that the data is clean or representative. A value can be present and still be inaccurate, out of date, biased, or recorded under an unclear definition. The unusual raw education and marriage codes are an example: there is a number in the cell, but the project still has to decide what that number means.</p>''',
'part-03-fundamentals.html':'''<h2>What happens while a model learns</h2><p>At first, a model makes poor guesses. Training gives it a row of features and the known label. A loss function puts a number on how wrong the prediction was. Optimisation changes the model's internal parameters so that similar mistakes become less common on the training data. Different algorithms make those changes differently. Logistic regression learns weights. A decision-tree model learns questions that split groups of rows. CatBoost builds many small trees one after another.</p><p>Training performance alone is not persuasive. A student can memorise a practice sheet and still fail a new exam. That is why the repository makes a train/test split. The test rows should act like the new exam. This project gets one important part right: the preprocessor is fitted on train rows and applied to test rows. It also has a later design issue: model choice compares test scores. Chapter 10 explains why that makes the final score less independent.</p><h3>Validation and cross-validation</h3><p>Validation is a rehearsal for the final test. In cross-validation, the training data is divided into folds. The system trains on some folds and validates on the remaining fold, rotating the held-out fold. A score averaged across rotations is less dependent on one lucky partition. The active trainer uses two folds. More folds can give a steadier estimate, though they cost more computation.</p><h3>Data leakage in plain English</h3><p>Leakage is an unfair hint from the answer sheet. A simple example: calculate the average of the entire dataset before the split, then use it to scale training rows. The training process has indirectly seen the test distribution. More serious examples include a column recorded after default, or a future payment. Leakage can produce impressive-looking scores that disappear in real use.</p>''',
'part-04-eda.html':'''<h2>How to look at a chart without guessing</h2><p>A histogram groups numbers into ranges. A tall bar means many records landed in that range. It does not mean the value is good or bad. A box plot gives a quick view of the middle of a distribution: the line is the median, the box covers the middle half, and points beyond the whiskers are possible outliers. In credit data, a large bill is not automatically an error. It may be a valid high-balance client, so removing it only because it looks extreme would be careless.</p><p>A heatmap colours a table of correlations. Near-zero correlation means there is little straight-line relationship in the two columns. A high positive number means both tend to rise together; a negative number means one tends to fall as the other rises. The model can still use a feature with weak correlation, because tree models can learn thresholds and interactions. Correlation also cannot answer "what causes default?"</p><h3>What the notebook did with EDA</h3><p>The UCI notebook checks raw quality, draws target and feature distributions, groups outcome rates by demographic/payment fields, and experiments with derived columns. It is useful for learning what the data contains. It is separate from the current automated training run. The root training code does not load the notebook's engineered CSV as its source; it reads the raw CSV and builds a different set of features in Python.</p><h3>How EDA should affect decisions</h3><p>EDA should generate questions rather than premature conclusions. The strong association between recent delay status and default gives the model a plausible signal to test. The long tails in bills and payments tell us to pay attention to scaling and outliers. The class distribution tells us to report recall and precision. None of those observations proves that a feature should be used without checking held-out performance, fairness, and data-collection policy.</p>''',
'part-05-preprocessing.html':'''<h2>Walk one client through the transformer</h2><p>Suppose a new client has a limit of 100,000, a latest bill of 50,000, and paid 10,000. The feature engineer can create utilisation of 0.50 and a latest payment ratio of 0.20. It also looks across six months to calculate averages, the largest repayment delay, and simple line slopes. These derived values do not add new outside information. They reorganise information already present in the six monthly fields into forms a model may find easier to use.</p><p>Next, the numerical pipeline fills a missing numerical value with the training-set median if one appears. The category pipeline fills a missing category with the most frequent training category. These are fallback rules. They are not evidence that a missing value genuinely equals the median or mode. In a real service, a missing or impossible user input often deserves a validation error instead of quiet imputation.</p><h3>Encoding and scaling slowly</h3><p>One-hot encoding turns a category into yes/no columns. After <code>drop='first'</code>, one category is used as a reference and is represented when the other category columns are all zero. Standard scaling changes the units so that, for example, an amount in hundreds of thousands does not numerically dwarf age. It is especially helpful for linear models. Tree models generally do not need it, but using the same transformed data keeps the six-model comparison consistent.</p><h3>Why save the preprocessor</h3><p>The saved CatBoost model was trained on a particular order of 42 transformed columns. If a new app creates columns differently, even a perfectly valid client profile can be interpreted incorrectly. Saving and reusing the preprocessor makes inference reproduce the same category mapping, median values, scaling numbers, feature formulas, and column order used during training.</p>''',
'part-06-imbalance.html':'''<h2>Use a concrete example</h2><p>Imagine 100 records with roughly this dataset's balance: 78 people do not default and 22 do. A lazy model that answers "no default" for all 100 is right 78 times. The accuracy looks respectable. It is useless for finding the 22 people in the class we are trying to identify. Recall exposes this problem because recall asks: out of all actual defaults, how many did we catch?</p><p>There is no universally correct balance technique. If a team lowers the threshold, it flags more potential defaults. That often increases recall, but also sends more people to review who would not default. If a team adds class weights, it tells the learning algorithm that a missed default counts more during fitting. If it uses SMOTE, it creates artificial training points between similar positive examples. Each choice changes the trade-off and needs to be measured on data that was not resampled.</p><h3>What your current code chose</h3><p>The active trainer did not use SMOTE, ADASYN, random sampling, or class weights. That is a valid baseline choice, but it has a visible consequence at the default cutoff: the verified model catches 472 of 1,313 defaults in the saved test split. It does not mean resampling would definitely be better. It means that the repository does not contain the experiment needed to support a claim either way.</p><h3>When accuracy is still useful</h3><p>Accuracy is not useless. It answers a real question: how often was the final hard label correct? It becomes dangerous when it is the only metric, especially when one class is more common. Read it alongside the entire confusion matrix, precision, recall, F1, ROC-AUC and, where relevant, average precision and business cost.</p>''',
'part-07-models.html':'''<h2>Why compare several models</h2><p>There is no rule that says one algorithm always wins on tabular credit data. Logistic regression gives a simple baseline. It assumes the log-odds can be represented by a weighted combination of features. A single decision tree can ask questions such as "is the recent delay at least two?" but can be unstable. Random Forest reduces that instability by averaging many varied trees. Boosting methods build trees in sequence, with later trees trying to improve earlier mistakes.</p><p>CatBoost is the winner saved in the current artifact. It is a gradient-boosting method, meaning it adds trees one at a time to reduce a loss. Each tree makes small corrections to the existing prediction. Depth controls how many levels a tree may use. Learning rate controls how strongly each new tree changes the combined score. More iterations allow more correction steps. These settings are trade-offs: bigger or deeper models may capture more detail, but can also learn noise.</p><h3>Reading feature importance safely</h3><p>The loaded model says PAY_0 has the largest internal importance. That is believable given the dataset and EDA, but it does not mean late payment directly causes default or that the value applies to every individual. Different importance methods can rank features differently, correlated features can share credit unpredictably, and importance says nothing about fairness. Use it as a lead for inspection, not as a customer-facing reason code.</p><h3>Models absent from the code</h3><p>Support vector machines, K-nearest neighbours, Naive Bayes, LightGBM and neural networks can be useful teaching comparisons. They were not candidates in the active trainer. In an interview, say what was tested, then explain how you would design a fair additional experiment instead of claiming a model was rejected.</p>''',
'part-08-pipeline.html':'''<h2>Training and prediction are different jobs</h2><p>Training happens occasionally. It reads known historical outcomes, learns preprocessing statistics and model parameters, then writes artifacts. Prediction happens repeatedly. It receives one client profile without a known target, applies the already learned preprocessor, and asks the saved model for a class and probability. Mixing these jobs is a common beginner mistake. A prediction endpoint must not refit the model or scaler on the incoming client.</p><p>The root project has a practical modular design. Ingestion owns file reading and splitting. Transformation owns feature construction. Training owns model selection. The prediction class owns artifact loading and scoring. The Flask form and JSON handler mainly translate user input into the exact 23-field DataFrame expected by the preprocessor. This separation makes the flow easier to trace and test.</p><h3>Why pipelines are more than neat organisation</h3><p>A pipeline turns a sequence of fragile manual steps into one object with a repeatable contract. It keeps fitting and transformation order consistent. A ColumnTransformer makes the contract explicit: these fields are numerical, these are categorical, and every remaining field is dropped. A stronger version would put the estimator in the same pipeline before cross-validation so each CV fold learns imputation, encoding and scaling from its own training portion.</p><h3>What inference returns</h3><p><code>predict()</code> gives the hard 0/1 decision using the model's current default threshold. <code>predict_proba()</code> gives two probabilities, one per class. The code selects column 1 as the default probability. The UI also groups the score into low, moderate and high display bands. Those bands help presentation, but they do not change the prediction threshold.</p>''',
'part-09-evaluation.html':'''<h2>Read the four matrix cells as stories</h2><p>A true negative is a client who did not default and was not flagged. A false positive is a client who did not default but was flagged. A false negative is a client who defaulted but was missed. A true positive is a client who defaulted and was flagged. The current saved confusion matrix has 841 false negatives and 238 false positives. Whether that is acceptable depends on what happens after a flag. Is there a human review? Does a flag harm a person? What is the cost of missing a default? The repository does not answer those policy questions.</p><p>Precision focuses on the flag: when the model says default, how often is it correct? Recall starts with real defaults: how many were found? F1 compresses both into one number, but it cannot replace the separate values because different use cases value different trade-offs. Specificity covers the other side, asking how well no-default cases are recognised.</p><h3>Why curves matter</h3><p>A hard threshold turns a smooth score into a yes/no decision. Move the threshold downward and more rows become positive. ROC-AUC avoids picking one threshold: it asks whether the model generally ranks a random default above a random non-default. A precision-recall curve is especially helpful when positives are less common because it keeps attention on the quality and coverage of positive flags.</p><h3>Probability quality</h3><p>Log loss rewards the model for assigning high probability to the event that actually occurred and penalises confident mistakes. Calibration checks whether the numerical scores match reality. A model can rank cases well and still be poorly calibrated. This project reports verified log loss, but it does not include a calibration study. That is why a displayed percentage should be described as the model's score, not as a guaranteed personal likelihood.</p>''',
'part-10-optimization.html':'''<h2>What GridSearchCV is doing behind the scenes</h2><p>A grid is simply a small menu of choices. For CatBoost, the active code tries two iteration counts, two depths and two learning rates. GridSearchCV makes each combination, trains it on one fold, scores it on the other, swaps the folds, and compares the score. The "best" setting means best under that chosen grid, score and fold arrangement. It does not mean mathematically optimal among every possible CatBoost configuration.</p><p>The same applies to model selection. The code does not prove CatBoost is always better than the other five algorithms. It proves that, in the saved run, CatBoost was the selected artifact after the specified small searches and test-score comparison. That is a precise and defensible statement.</p><h3>The test-set issue explained</h3><p>A final test set should be like a sealed exam paper. If you look at the scores of six candidates on it and select the highest one, the selection process has learned something from that exam. The winning score can be slightly lucky. A better workflow uses only training data and cross-validation or a validation split to choose the algorithm and settings. Then it opens the test set once to report the final unbiased estimate.</p><h3>Useful next experiments</h3><p>Use a stratified split so positive proportion stays stable. Try a larger, justified search space. Compare average precision, recall at a fixed review capacity, calibration, and cost-sensitive metrics. Record every run with data version, seed, features, parameters and scores. These are recommendations. They are not currently implemented.</p>''',
'part-11-code.html':'''<h2>Read code by following the data</h2><p>Begin at <code>train_pipeline.py</code>. It creates a DataIngestion object, then calls its method. That method uses pandas <code>read_csv</code> to make a DataFrame, then sklearn's <code>train_test_split</code> to create two DataFrames and write them to artifact CSV files. The transformation component reads those two files again, separates the target column from the feature columns, and turns feature tables into numerical arrays. The trainer receives arrays where the final column is the target.</p><p>This style can look intimidating because it uses classes, dataclasses and imports. You can translate it into simple questions: what value goes into this function? What does it return? Does it change the data, fit something, save something, or call another component? For example, <code>CustomData.get_data_as_data_frame()</code> returns a one-row table with exact column names. It does not predict anything. The prediction method takes that table and performs the saved transformations.</p><h3>Files that are easy to confuse</h3><p><code>src/pipeline/tempCodeRunnerFile.py</code> looks like an editor scratch file and is not the active inference import. The project also has several copied or experimental folders. Some contain incompatible fraud wording or unrelated older code. The active root imports and artifact paths are the reliable guide: <code>src/</code>, <code>artifacts/</code>, <code>application.py</code>, <code>api/predict.py</code>, and the root raw CSV path.</p><h3>Persistence</h3><p>Pickle serialisation writes a Python object to a file and reads it back later. It is convenient for a local trusted project. Never load unknown pickle files from the internet because pickle loading can execute malicious code. In a mature deployment, you also record package versions, artifact checksums, model version, data version and compatibility tests.</p>''',
'part-12-production.html':'''<h2>What changes when people rely on a prediction</h2><p>A notebook or local form can assume friendly inputs and a developer watching the screen. A real service cannot. It needs to reject impossible ages, unknown category codes, missing fields, huge payloads and unauthorised requests. It needs safe error messages that help the user without exposing internal stack traces. It also needs to keep personal financial information out of logs unless there is a lawful, protected reason to retain it.</p><p>Deployment is not the same as copying a pickle file to a server. The model, preprocessor and input schema must travel together as a versioned unit. Every prediction should be traceable to a model version and policy version. Monitoring has to check whether new client inputs have changed, whether actual outcomes still match predictions, and whether scores remain calibrated. Once real outcomes arrive, the team needs a controlled retraining process rather than silently replacing a model.</p><h3>Human and legal safeguards</h3><p>Credit decisions can affect housing, work, health and financial security. Demographic fields and historical patterns can reproduce unfair treatment even when a model has a good aggregate metric. A production setting needs legal review, fairness testing, explainability appropriate to the decision, human oversight, adverse-action procedures where required, consent/privacy controls and an appeal route. The current repository does not implement these controls.</p><h3>A realistic next milestone</h3><p>Before any external use, build a small internal-only service with strict schema validation, authentication, safe logging, a versioned pipeline, test coverage, a monitored dashboard and a human-review-only workflow. Then evaluate the data, policy and fairness questions with the people responsible for risk, law, privacy and operations.</p>'''
}

for file,extra in deep.items():
    p=root/file
    text=p.read_text(encoding='utf8')
    marker='<h2>Check understanding</h2>'
    if 'Start with the everyday picture' not in text and marker in text:
        p.write_text(text.replace(marker,extra+marker),encoding='utf8')
